handoff:

The values column is documentation: nothing checks that effort is one of S,
M, L. Making it a check is what forces the file's shape, because the only
validator worth having is a regex, and a regex cannot live in a markdown
table — it alternates on | and the table separates cells on |. That is the
container's fault, not the regex's, and the design already predicted it: the
table holds while the schema stays flat key/value, and per-value behaviour
forces something else.

So the file becomes the format's own grammar applied to its own
configuration, and loses the extension for the same reason info has none —
the content is key: value lines no renderer understands, and a .md suffix
invites an editor to reflow it.

.pidly/schema:

  field: effort
  match: S|M|L
  note: rough size

  field: area
  required: yes
  match: format|cli|docs
  note: which part of the project

  field: due
  match: [0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}
  note: a date, if the work has one

  field: handoff
  note: where the last agent got to

Stanzas separated by a blank line, field: opens one, declaration order is the
order new writes slots in. Nothing after "match: " is a delimiter, so a
pattern can hold |, spaces, brackets and braces with no escaping. Read with
the idioms already in use:

  awk '$1 == "field:" { print $2 }' .pidly/schema
  awk '$1=="field:" { cur=$2 } $1=="required:" && $2=="yes" { print cur }' \
      .pidly/schema

Semantics:

- match: is a POSIX ERE, evaluated anchored — pidly tests ^(<pattern>)$, so
  S|M|L cannot half-match "M extra", and a pattern the author anchored
  already still works.
- No match: means anything, which is what a values cell holding prose means
  today.
- required: stays and is not folded into the pattern. match is shape,
  required is presence; collapsing them makes every optional field carry a
  trailing ? and read like a mistake. An empty value skips the match and is
  a finding only when required: yes.
- note: is one line, per handoff.

Two portability findings, both of which the implementation has to respect.
Tested 2026-09-17 on aarch64 Linux with mawk 1.3.4 20200120 (/usr/bin/awk
and nawk are both that binary) and ugrep 7.8.4 as grep:

- awk cannot be the matcher. [0-9]{4} matches 2026 under grep -E and does
  not under mawk 1.3.4, which has no interval expressions, in a literal
  /re/ or in a dynamic $0 ~ p. gawk does support them, which is the point:
  the pattern's meaning must not depend on which awk is installed. Patterns
  go through grep -Eq, never through one of check's existing awk passes.
- A permissive grep hides a dialect error. ugrep 7.8.4 accepted \d, where
  GNU grep -E treats it as undefined, so a pattern written against whatever
  is installed locally can behave differently on the next machine. check
  lints the pattern for \d \w \s \b and (?...) and reports them rather
  than trusting the local tool. A pattern grep cannot compile exits 2,
  distinguishable from 1 for no-match, so a broken schema is a finding
  rather than a crash.

Both are properties of the tools, not of this checkout, so re-test them
wherever the work happens: awk -W version, grep --version, and the two
one-liners above.

Findings check gains:

  7: effort is 'XL', which does not match S|M|L
  schema: field 'effort' has a match grep cannot compile
  schema: 'due' uses \d, which is not ERE
  schema: a stanza with no field:

No migration path. The project is new and installed nowhere else, so init
writes .pidly/schema and nothing reads schema.md — this repo's own file is
converted as part of the work, and so are the fifteen references in the
script, five in the design, two in the README and thirteen across the
suites.

This absorbs 26, which asked for the prose to come out of schema.md: the new
file has nowhere to put prose except a note: line per field, and pidly's own
created and updated are not declared in it at all.

Done when .pidly/schema is the seeded file, values are validated against
anchored EREs through grep, check reports the four findings above, the
reference and AGENTS block name the new file, and the tests cover a value
outside its pattern, an empty optional field, an empty required one, a
pattern containing |, a pattern grep cannot compile, and a pattern using a
PCRE escape.


